\section{A prediction is a distribution}{

Every prediction \code{limn} makes is a predictive distribution: a mixture, over
draws of the fitted parameters, of the model family's distribution for the
response. Intervals, quantiles and scores are all read from that mixture.
Scoring it directly, rather than scoring responses simulated from it, removes
the simulation's noise from the score.
}

\section{Scores are losses}{

Every score \code{limn} reports is a loss, so lower is better: the log score is
the negative log predictive density, and the CRPS, Brier, pinball, interval,
squared and absolute scores are penalties.
}

\section{The workflow}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Predict.} \code{\link[=ilm_predict]{ilm_predict()}} gives the distribution of a mean, a new
observation or the linear predictor, for a unit the fit has seen, a new
one, a typical one or the average over units. \code{\link[=ilm_forecast]{ilm_forecast()}} carries
series forward in time. \code{\link[=ilm_future]{ilm_future()}} builds the rows a forecast needs,
and \code{\link[=ilm_covariate_paths]{ilm_covariate_paths()}} and \code{\link[=ilm_as_draws]{ilm_as_draws()}} give covariates that are
not known in advance as draws.
\item \strong{Combine.} \code{\link[=ilm_aggregate]{ilm_aggregate()}} totals a prediction over rows, and
\code{\link[=ilm_average]{ilm_average()}} takes weighted averages of means (standardisation and
post-stratification).
\item \strong{Check against outcomes.} \link[=ilm_scores.ilm_pred]{ilm_scores()} scores
predictions by proper scoring rules; \code{\link[=ilm_pit]{ilm_pit()}} and \code{\link[=ilm_plot_pit]{ilm_plot_pit()}} show
where outcomes fell in their distributions; and
\link[=ilm_calibration.ilm_pred]{ilm_calibration()} gives a verdict on
calibration, with \code{\link[=ilm_plot_calibration]{ilm_plot_calibration()}}.
\item \strong{Validate out of sample.} \code{\link[=ilm_folds]{ilm_folds()}} builds folds that follow the
data's structure, and \code{\link[=ilm_cv]{ilm_cv()}} scores a model on them.
\item \strong{Compare models.} \code{\link[=ilm_compare]{ilm_compare()}} compares scores with standard errors
that respect how the data hang together; \code{\link[=ilm_plot_compare]{ilm_plot_compare()}} and
\code{\link[=ilm_plot_horizon]{ilm_plot_horizon()}} draw the comparison.
\item \strong{Draw.} \code{\link[=ilm_plot_fan]{ilm_plot_fan()}} draws forecasts as fan charts.
\item \strong{Extend.} \code{\link[=ilm_predict_draws]{ilm_predict_draws()}} and \code{\link[=ilm_cv_refit]{ilm_cv_refit()}} let a package that
fits illume's models another way supply the draws and the refits, so that
every function above works on its fits.
}
}

\section{Checks, and what to do about them}{

Every check \code{limn} makes takes its words from one registry. Each says what
was found, what it means, and what to do about it, and gives the source it
rests on, or says that the reasoning is \code{limn}'s own. A check is warned when
it is raised, with class \verb{lmn_<id>}, and kept on the result in \code{checks}, so
it can be read as data as well as words. Where the fix is a change to the
model, the check names the call: \code{illume::ilm_remedies()} lists the changes
the fit's own checks support, and \code{illume::ilm_apply_remedy()} refits the
model with the one you choose.
}

